  "coverage": "Implemented formulas have explicit item membership and numeric source evidence. Remaining expressions, diagnostic algorithms, percentiles, custom missing-answer policies and interpretation require review.",
  "rules_provenance": null,
  "source_rule_excerpts": [
   "A normative percentile is calculated, contextualising the responses in comparison to typical patterns of responding among Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islanders (Brinkley et al., 2021; Cunningham et al., 2012). For example, a percentile of 50 indicates average (and healthy) levels of distress, while a percentile of 90 indicates the respondent has more distress than 90% of the indigenous population.",
   "A cut off score of 11 and above is indicative of clinically significant psychological distress, such as depression or anxiety symptoms (Brinckley, 2021)."
  ],
  "missing_item_policy": {
   "origin": "application_policy",
   "action": "withhold_result_if_any_required_response_is_missing",
   "note": "Conservative prototype policy, not a claim that the source prohibits prorating. Source exceptions require an explicit reviewed rule."
  },
  "percentile_rules": [],
  "percentile_status": "not_implemented_no_unverified_lookup",
  "limitations": [
   "Rules do not establish a diagnosis.",
   "No normative percentile is inferred from sample results or approximate distribution data."
  ],
  "source_guidance": [
   {
    "title": "K5 Scoring & Interpretation",
    "text": "A total score is calculated by summing all items for a possible range of 5 to 25. Higher scores indicate higher levels of psychological distress.\n\nFour categories of psychological distress were outlined by the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare (AIHW, 2009), and include:\nLow (5–7)\nModerate (8–11)\nHigh (12–14)\nVery High (15–25)\n\nA normative percentile is calculated, contextualising the responses in comparison to typical patterns of responding among Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islanders (Brinkley et al., 2021; Cunningham et al., 2012). For example, a percentile of 50 indicates average (and healthy) levels of distress, while a percentile of 90 indicates the respondent has more distress than 90% of the indigenous population.\n\nA cut off score of 11 and above is indicative of clinically significant psychological distress, such as depression or anxiety symptoms (Brinckley, 2021).\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
